  "result": "I've written the revised record for `LVMC_11_43_010_B_o1` to `record.jsonl`. It hasn't been through the gate yet; the harness will check it next.\n\n**Cause of the refusal:** span `b6` (\"shall yield\") claimed `kin.speed`, but the node's obligation never gives speed a role. Yielding is judged at the entry by two things: no contact with a vehicle already in the roundabout, and no near-collision course with one. A faithful rule therefore can't read `kin.speed`, and the gate returned `UNUSED_CLAIM`.\n\n**Changes:**\n- **`b6`:** now claims only `rel.contact`. Its justification says the \"danger of a crash\" part comes from the `b7` right-of-way standard (the closest-approach checks), and why speed is not claimed.\n- **Obligation text:** now states that the duty holds at every roundabout the subject enters there. This matches how the coder wrote the rule.\n- **`b6` narrowing:** now says the subject's speed is not itself judged.\n\nEverything else is unchanged: the other spans, parameters, the right-of-way standard and the scenarios. In the coder's diagnostic run, the only failure left was the unused `kin.speed` claim; the rule and all 8 witnesses passed otherwise.\n\n**Risk:** the run's shared mapping index pairs \"shall yield\" with both `kin.speed` and `rel.contact`. The validator may flag `b6` as inconsistent with it. The repair rules say to drop a claim no faithful rule can use, so if it is flagged, the index entry is what needs revisiting, not this record.",
